  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They held the lead for four innings and lost it in one. The bullpen couldn't survive the fifth.",
    "what_this_means": "The game turned on a stretch of damage the Padres couldn't repair. They took the lead early. Scoring twice in the first. And held it through four complete innings. Then Toronto scored four in the fifth, and that was the game. Brito gave up two runs in 2.2 innings after Sears exited at 4.1. It was the same problem as always: good enough to stay close, not good enough to finish. They got runners on base seven times with runners in scoring position and converted exactly once. The math on that doesn't work against anyone. Dur\u00e1n went 2-for-2 and it meant nothing because the rest of the lineup left six on. One run on seven RISP chances is not competing. They never made it matter.",
    "what_to_watch": "Trey Yesavage takes the mound for Toronto tonight with a 3.31 ERA, and the Blue Jays rank among baseball's weakest offenses. This is the kind of matchup the Padres have to win.",
